# snap.yaml TEMPLATE for the Lightning snap. build-snap.sh substitutes
# @VERSION@ and packs the tree with mksquashfs.
#
# Why not snapcraft: snapcraft is distributed only as a snap and its
# build-snaps/base handling requires a running snapd, which the
# Docker-outside-of-Docker runner fleet cannot provide, and the core24
# Ubuntu archive Qt (6.4) is older than Lightning's minimum (6.5). A snap is
# a squashfs with meta/snap.yaml, so the pipeline packs the fully bundled
# AppDir produced by the AppImage job (Debian 13 Qt) directly. Strict
# confinement is DECLARED with the interfaces below; because no snapd can
# run inside this fleet, confinement behaviour is validated structurally in
# CI, not against a live snapd.
#
# AND STRUCTURAL VALIDATION MISSED THAT THIS SNAP COULD NOT START AT ALL.
# Run for the first time under a real snapd on Ubuntu 24.04 (2026-09-13) it
# failed three ways before showing a window, and every one of them is
# invisible to a check that asks "is the file present":
#
#   1. Qt could not reach the compositor. snapd remaps XDG_RUNTIME_DIR to
#      $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/snap.lightning and the Wayland socket stays one level
#      up, so the relative WAYLAND_DISPLAY every session sets resolved to
#      nothing. snapcraft's desktop-launch bridges it; this hand-written
#      launcher did not. Fixed in build-snap.sh's launcher.
#   2. No GL. The payload carries libEGL.so.1/libGL.so.1 because `ldd` sees
#      them, but those are glvnd DISPATCH stubs -- the vendor driver is
#      dlopened and nothing staged it, and core24 has no Mesa. Under strict
#      confinement /usr is the base snap's, so the host's Mesa is unreachable
#      BY CONSTRUCTION. Qt fell back to its software renderer, which cannot
#      draw video at all: no call video, no screen share. Fixed by the
#      gpu-2404 content plug below.
#   3. It then SEGFAULTED, because core24 carries no xkb keymaps and no
#      fontconfig configuration. Both are staged now. FONTS are NOT: their
#      search path is absolute, so a tree under $SNAP is inert, and snapd's
#      `desktop` interface already bind-mounts the host's font directories.
#   4. The microphone reported "Connection refused" and the pipeline cascaded
#      into "srtpenc0: Could not initialize SRTP encoder" -- the PipeWire and
#      Pulse sockets sit one level up from the remapped runtime dir exactly as
#      the compositor socket does. Same bridge, same cause.
#
# GENERALISE, for the fifth time in this repository: a dependency that is
# DLOPENED is invisible to `ldd`, so "the library is staged" is not "the
# feature works". Only running the shipped artefact the way a user runs it
# answers the question.
name: lightning
version: "@VERSION@"
summary: Native Matrix desktop client
description: |
  Lightning is a native C++ and Qt Matrix desktop client with the official
  Matrix Rust SDK backend. GPL-3.0-or-later.
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
base: core24
grade: stable
confinement: strict
architectures:
  - amd64
apps:
  lightning:
    command: bin/lightning-launch
    plugs:
      - network
      - network-bind
      - wayland
      - x11
      - desktop
      - desktop-legacy
      - opengl
      - audio-playback
      # Voice/video calling. audio-playback covers only what the user HEARS;
      # the microphone is a separate interface, so without this a call is
      # one-directional silence. It is not auto-connected on every system, so
      # a user may still have to `snap connect lightning:audio-record`.
      - audio-record
      # v4l2src opens /dev/video* directly (autovideosrc is deliberately not
      # used -- on a PipeWire desktop pipewiresrc outranks v4l2src and
      # produces no frames), so the camera needs this interface rather than
      # the portal. Screen sharing takes the portal route instead and rides
      # on the desktop interface above.
      - camera
      # libsecret / Secret Service access for token storage.
      - password-manager-service
      - gpu-2404

# GRAPHICS, FROM CANONICAL'S MESA CONTENT SNAP.
#
# A content plug rather than staging Mesa ourselves: the driver stack is
# large, it has to match the kernel's DRM, and it gains security fixes on its
# own schedule -- all three are arguments for letting the platform ship it.
# The provider mounts at $SNAP/gpu-2404 and carries a wrapper that sets
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH, __EGL_VENDOR_LIBRARY_DIRS and the dri driver path before
# exec'ing the app; the launcher goes through it when it is present and falls
# back to a direct exec when it is not, so an unconnected snap still starts
# (on the software renderer, with the app's own warning).
#
# `default-provider` IS NOT AUTO-CONNECTION, and reading it as such would
# ship the defect this plug exists to fix. snapd's base declaration for
# `content` allows auto-connection only when the plug snap's publisher equals
# the slot snap's; mesa-2404 is Canonical's and Lightning is not. So the
# store will INSTALL mesa-2404 alongside and then leave the interface
# disconnected, and a user who does nothing runs on the software renderer
# with no call video at all. Until a store snap-declaration grants it, the
# connection is manual and the install instructions have to say so -- as they
# already must for `camera`, `audio-record` and `password-manager-service`,
# which are manual for the same reason:
#
#   snap connect lightning:gpu-2404 mesa-2404:gpu-2404
#   snap connect lightning:audio-record
#   snap connect lightning:camera
#   snap connect lightning:password-manager-service
plugs:
  gpu-2404:
    interface: content
    target: $SNAP/gpu-2404
    default-provider: mesa-2404
